From 5f1c3f5f2b133c6ac904a013383c5996bd64a995 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenWCH Phase 3 <phase3@openwch.local>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 01:38:59 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 2/7] RISC-V: emit WCH XW compressed EVT load/store bytes

Add the eight observed XW compressed byte/halfword load and store
encodings, including GAS aliases that compress ordinary lbu/lhu/sb/sh.
Keep the WCH initial-arch eligibility rule and preserve its disassembler
behavior by printing these private encodings as .insn.

INSN_CLASS_XW deliberately gets no row in the shared BFD subset tables.
Both users decide the class before those tables are consulted: the
assembler tests the sticky initial-architecture flag in its own candidate
loop, and the disassembler skips the class outright, so a row in
riscv_multi_subset_supports or riscv_multi_subset_supports_ext would be
unreachable.

The xlen_requirement column stays 0.  WCH's assembler takes every one of
these forms under -march=rv64imac_xw and emits the same halfwords it emits
for rv32, and its disassembler reads them back out of an elf64 object, so
pinning the column to 32 would diverge from it on four surfaces at once.
The quietest of the four emits no diagnostic at all: with the rows filtered
out on rv64 the lbu/lhu/sb/sh aliases still assemble, just as their
four-byte base forms, and the object silently grows.

Behavior specification:
  analysis/toolchain/wch-gcc-toolchain-survey.md sections 5.3 and 5.4
  ref/wch-isa-research/isa/custom/wch-custom-isa-reference.md sections
  3.1 (slots, operands and the immediate mappings) and 3.2 (the F/C/D/Zcb
  exclusivity boundary)
  tmp/phase9-evidence/15.2.0/spec.md (the rv64 acceptance, encoding,
  diagnostic and disassembly matrix measured against the WCH 15.2.0
  binaries: 184 probes a side, rv32 and rv64 paired column by column)
